## 1. Starting endpoint and implication chains

The previous candidate [direction-cone report](global_contact_direction_cone_caps_2026-09-26.md)
reduces an additional, whole-positive-contact direction hypothesis to four inactive
caps and a common line cut for all positive diameter edges. In the canonical
normalization this leaves a macroscopic-container caterpillar forest, with the
diameter-carrying core still requiring replacement or release. The hypothesis
has not been proved for arbitrary global sources.

Two bridges were pursued:

```text
whole positive-contact direction width <= pi/3
 -> one monotone positive-contact path
 -> unique diameter pair and a straight unit chain
 -> one-dimensional maximal force hull if the stress is maximal
 -> canonical exclusion of this exact angular endpoint.
```

```text
common line cut for all positive diameter pairs
 -> nonnegative electrical cross-transfer (proposed)
 -> contradiction with C-ZTA
 -> elimination of the surviving macroscopic forest (failed).
```

The second bridge is false, even for exact planar unit contacts, normalized KKT
equilibrium, and a PSD scalar stress. Section 4 supplies its complete certificate.
No same-cardinality source competitor follows from either bridge.

## 2. The contact-arc theorem AC60

Let A be a finite triangle-free unit-separated planar source, D=diam(A)>1.
Choose a normalized nonnegative KKT stress with positive contact set C+, positive
diameter set H+, and positive-contact vertex set V. Assume the positive-contact
graph on V is connected. Suppose all its unoriented edge directions lie in one
closed projective arc of width W <= pi/3. Then:

1. V is a straight consecutive unit chain with D edges; in particular D is an integer.
2. H+ consists of its unique endpoint pair.
3. Every positive contact weight is D and the diameter weight is one.

This is an exact selected-source-support statement. It does not assume global
optimality, canonical normalization, a lattice scaffold, a minimum weight, or a
PSD stress. Source points outside V remain in A.

### Complete proof

Rotate coordinates so the projective arc is represented by oriented angles in
I=[0,W]. Every positive contact vector has its oriented angle in I or I+pi.
These two intervals are disjoint and each is contained in an open half-plane.

There cannot be two distinct unit neighbors of a vertex in the same interval.
Their mutual distance is 2 sin(theta/2) <= 1, where theta <= W <= pi/3 is
the angle between their outgoing unit rays. If the distance is smaller than
one, separation fails. If it equals one, the vertex and its two neighbors form
an actual unit equilateral triangle. This is forbidden, whether or not the
neighbor-neighbor contact has positive multiplier. Thus each vertex has at
most one outgoing positive contact ray in each interval, and degree at most two.

Orient a simple path consecutively. If an incoming step belongs to I, its
negative outgoing ray at the shared vertex belongs to I+pi. The next step
cannot belong to I+pi, by the previous paragraph. Thus every step on that
path belongs to I. The same argument works with I and I+pi interchanged.
Every cycle would consequently have all its consecutive steps in one open
half-plane and could not close. A finite connected graph of maximum degree
two with an edge and without a cycle is a path. Write its vertices as
p0,...,pk and its unit steps as u1,...,uk, all oriented in I after reversing
the path if necessary.

For every i,j, ui dot uj >= cos W >= 1/2. Extending any nonempty sum of
consecutive steps by one adjacent step increases its squared norm by
1 + 2 sum(ui dot uj) > 0. Therefore the unique farthest pair on the path
is p0,pk.

Every endpoint p of a positive diameter edge pq belongs to V. Indeed, if p
had no positive contact, its equilibrium would say
sum_r beta_pr (p-r)=0 over its positive diameter neighbors. For each such r,

    (p-q) dot (p-r) = D^2 - |q-r|^2/2 >= D^2/2 > 0.

Taking the dot product contradicts equilibrium. Since H+ is nonempty and
all of its endpoints are in V, diam(V)=D. The unique farthest-pair result
therefore forces H+={p0,pk} and |pk-p0|=D.

Each interior vertex pj is unloaded. Its two-contact equilibrium is
alpha_j uj = alpha_(j+1) u_(j+1). The vectors are unit and the multipliers
are strictly positive, so alpha_j=alpha_(j+1) and uj=u_(j+1).
All steps are the same unit vector. Consequently D=k. Normalization sets
the sole diameter multiplier to one, and endpoint equilibrium sets the
common contact multiplier to D. This proves every assertion.

### Exact canonical consequence and scope correction

Canonical C12 gives connected positive-contact support for every normalized
KKT stress of a global source. Therefore AC60 applies to such a selected stress
whenever its directions satisfy the stated hypothesis.

If the stress has **maximal support**, AC60 makes its force hull a segment.
The canonical one-dimensional maximal-force-hull exclusion rules this out for
D>1. Hence the positive contact directions of maximal support in a triangle-free
global source cannot fit in a projective arc of width at most pi/3. In particular
the same necessary condition holds for the canonical full-support C-II stress.
It also holds for the full actual contact direction set of the original source,
because this contains the maximal positive contact set.

We do not assert this exclusion for every selected stress. The restoration
audit explicitly allows the selected straight-chain calculation while excluding
the one-dimensional **maximal** force hull. Confusing those scopes would be an
invalid promotion. The proof above correctly characterizes the selected case.

### Dependencies

- Canonical KKT equilibrium, [shared local structure](../theory/01-local-structure.md).
- Canonical C12 and the maximal-force-hull exclusion,
  [force-hull module, Sections 2 and 6](../targets/target-c/01-force-hull-and-shear.md).
- Scope clarification, [C53 restoration audit, Section 4](target_c_c53_orientation_and_ci_restoration_2026-09-09.md).
- Full-support normalization only for the C-II corollary,
  [C-I restoration](../targets/target-c/56-ci-restored-frontier.md).

The graph and distance arguments in AC60 itself are proved above and require
none of these global results beyond the stated KKT data.

## 3. Sharpness above pi/3 without global minimality

For any integer k>=3 let c=1/2-1/k^2, s=sqrt(1-c^2), u=(1,0), v=(c,s).
For any integer m>=2 take

    V_m = {iu : -m<=i<=m} union {iv : -m<=i<=m}.

The shared origin is counted once. The projective contact width is
theta=arccos(c)>pi/3, tending to pi/3 as k tends to infinity.
The actual contacts are precisely consecutive pairs on the two chains.
For mixed nonzero indices their squared distance is
i^2+j^2-2cij. For opposite signs it exceeds two. For equal signs,

    i^2+j^2-2cij = (|i|-|j|)^2 + 2(1-c)|ij| > 1.

Thus all separations hold and there is no unit triangle. The diameter is 2m,
attained exactly by the opposite endpoints of each chain: all points are in
the radius-m disk and no mixed endpoints are antipodal. Put alpha=m on
each of its 4m contacts and beta=1/2 on each of its two diameters.
Interior forces cancel; at either endpoint, m times its outward unit contact
equals one half of its diameter vector. At the origin the four forces cancel.
This is normalized positive KKT equilibrium on a connected nonstraight core.

Its scalar stress is also PSD. The quadratic form is the sum, over the two
chains, of

    m sum_(2m steps) (delta f)^2 - (f_end-f_start)^2/2 >= 0

by the inequality (sum_(2m) delta f)^2 <= 2m sum_(2m)(delta f)^2.
Equality requires f affine along each chain. The shared origin leaves exactly
three independent scalar parameters, so the kernel is the affine coordinate
kernel on this noncollinear core. Thus PSD and an affine scalar kernel do not
extend AC60 through any fixed positive angular excess.

These sources are not asserted globally minimizing. Their diameter count two
violates the canonical large-source h>=ceil(D) requirement when m>=2.
Their mixed-framework rank and global optimality are omitted; this is exactly
the hypothesis distinction that a valid continuation must exploit.

## 4. The failed line-cut transfer bridge: exact hexagon

Use the regular unit hexagon p_i=(cos(i*pi/3),sin(i*pi/3)), i=0,...,5.
Its actual unit contacts are the six cycle edges, and D=2 with actual diameter
pairs (0,3),(1,4),(2,5). Set alpha=2/3 on every contact and beta=1/3 on
every diameter. At p_i the contact reaction is (2/3)p_i and the diameter
reaction is (1/3)(2p_i), so equilibrium is exact.

Take t=(1,sqrt(3)). The t-coordinates of p0,p1,p2 are 1,2,1; those of
p3,p4,p5 are -1,-2,-1. All three diameter pairs cross the same line and
are coherently oriented from its positive side to its negative side.

Let L_C be the contact Laplacian and Q have columns e0-e3,e1-e4,e2-e5.
Ground vertex 5. Direct rational inversion gives

             [ 9/4   3/4  -3/4 ]
    K=Q^T L_C^+ Q = [ 3/4   9/4   3/4 ].
             [-3/4   3/4   9/4 ]

The inverse on mean-zero vectors and the grounded inverse give the same K
because every column of Q has total zero. In particular K_13=-3/4.
The scalar stress Omega=L_C-L_H is symmetric and satisfies Omega^2=2 Omega
and trace(Omega)=6. Its eigenvalues are zero and two, with rank three. It is
PSD with the affine kernel. The script verifies these identities in rational
arithmetic and checks every pairwise contact and diameter constraint.

The regular-hexagon response is already a binding canonical example in the C35/C50
audits. We claim no new hexagon configuration or general response theorem.
The new failed implication tested here is specifically the attempted use of our
common-line-cut conclusion as a substitute for a transfer sign theorem.

This example has contact projective directions 0,pi/3,2pi/3, so it does not
refute a theorem using the additional width <pi/2 contact hypothesis. Its D=2
also lies below the canonical C-ZTA regime. It does decisively refute the
unqualified sign shortcut from a line cut, unit separation, KKT, and PSD.

## 5. Exact remaining lemma and frontier effect

AC60 removes the exact W<=pi/3 endpoint on maximal support. Combined with
the previous candidate it leaves, under that report's extra acute-direction
hypothesis, only

    pi/3 < W < pi/2,

with four macroscopic inactive regions, a common line cut, and canonical
caterpillar-forest diameter support. This is a conditional narrowing of the
original C-II macrocontainer obstruction, not its elimination.

One exact possible next theorem is:

> For the canonical full-support C-II source, if all positive contact directions
> fit in one projective arc of width W<pi/2, then all coherently oriented
> diameter-pair transfer entries K_ef are nonnegative.

Such a theorem would contradict canonical C-ZTA for D>=9. It must use the
actual unit-contact geometry, the strict acute arc, canonical normalization,
and all diameter constraints; the common line cut is insufficient. Its truth
has not been established here. The crossed-chain family has zero cross-transfer
and so does not refute it, but fails canonical mixed rigidity and h>=ceil(D).

The other exact remaining lemma is source-preserving replacement or release of
the diameter-carrying core while retaining all n source points, all separations,
and a strictly smaller global diameter. Refilling only the inactive caps while
keeping the old core cannot meet that conclusion.

## 6. Claim and regression audit

| Claim | Classification | Decisive scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| AC60 | proved/unreviewed candidate | connected selected positive core, all its contact directions |
| W>pi/3 for maximal support | candidate canonical consequence | uses maximal force hull exclusion |
| W>pi/3 for every selected stress | not claimed | canonical restoration distinguishes selected chains |
| any fixed extension of AC60 using KKT+PSD alone | rejected | near-threshold crossed chains |
| common line cut implies K_ef>=0 | rejected | exact hexagon K_13=-3/4 |
| acute canonical source implies K_ef>=0 | open | hexagon fails acute arc, cross fails canonical rigidity |
| Level I closure | absent | arbitrary sources and core replacement remain unresolved |

The proof handles equality at 60 degrees through an **actual** source triangle,
not a triangle of selected positive contacts. Degree-two chains, source points
outside the core, and positive weights without lower bounds are retained.
Stars cannot evade the degree bound at W<=pi/3. The threshold witnesses have
four-ray stars precisely because their same-half angle is strictly larger.
No external report from another current PR is used as a dependency.

## 7. Computation and validation

Run `python3 research/verify_contact_arc_threshold.py`.
The proposition tested is the existence of a negative cross-transfer with an
actual common line cut, and feasibility/KKT of near-threshold crossed chains.
Arithmetic is Fraction throughout; points use one formal radical whose square
is rational, making every squared distance rational. The exact outputs include
K_13=-3/4 for the hexagon and neighbor distance squared 5001/5000 for k=100.
The program checks all pairwise constraints, full contact and diameter sets,
normalized forces, and the displayed PSD matrix identity for the hexagon.
The all-m,k chain PSD proof and AC60 are analytic, not inferred from samples.

No computation certifies global optimality. No literature theorem was imported.

## 8. Promotion recommendation and handoff

Preserve as **unreviewed**, and obtain independent audit of AC60 and the maximal
support scope before promotion. Do not change canonical state or Level I status.
The strongest new theorem is the exact contact-arc-to-straight-chain implication.
Its canonical chain is C12 -> AC60 -> maximal-force-hull exclusion.
The transfer bridge is dead without additional acute geometry. Read this report,
the previous direction-cone report, C-ZTA, and the C53 selected/maximal scope
paragraph before continuing. The next attack must confront 60-degree excess
using canonical mixed rigidity or prove a valid acute-contact transfer theorem.
